(Y Test Configurations) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 704.64 Tm
/F5 16.0 Tf
(Y.1 Setup for Terminals) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 675.08 Tm
/F5 14.0 Tf
(Y.1.1   Common Setup for All Terminals) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 653.68 Tm
/F9 10.0 Tf
(The preferred acoustic access to LTE, 5G NR, and WLAN terminals is the most realistic simulation of the "average") Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 641.28 Tm
(subscriber. This can be made by using HATS \(head and torso simulator\), with appropriate ear simulation and) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 628.88 Tm
(appropriate mountings of handset terminals to the HATS in a realistic but reproducible way. Hands-free terminals) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 616.48 Tm
(shall use the HATS or free field microphone techniques in a realistic but reproducible way.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 595.08 Tm
(HATS is described in ITU-T Recommendation P.58 [15], appropriate ears are described in ITU-T Recommendation) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 582.68 Tm
(P.57 [14] \(Type 3.3\), proper positioning of handsets in realistic conditions is found in ITU-T Recommendation P.64,) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 570.28 Tm
(and the test setups for various types of hands-free terminals can be found in ITU-T Recommendation P.581.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 548.88 Tm
(Unless stated otherwise, if a volume control is provided, the setting is chosen such that the nominal RLR is met as) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 536.48 Tm
(close as possible.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 515.08 Tm
(The preferred way of testing is the connection of a terminal to the system simulator with exact defined settings and) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 502.68 Tm
(access points. The test sequences are fed in either electrically using a reference codec, using the direct signal) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 490.28 Tm
(processing approach, or acoustically using ITU-T specified devices.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 468.88 Tm
(The system simulator shall simulate the access network and core network including the immersive audio) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 456.48 Tm
(encoding/decoding specified for the test \(i.e. IVAS\) but excluding further transcoding beyond linear PCM.) Tj
1 0 0 1 86.20 435.08 Tm
1.000 0.000 0.000 rg
(Editor\221s Note:      IVAS may not yet be available for the specification of test configurations. During this period) Tj
1 0 0 1 128.75 422.68 Tm
(linear PCM coding of the signals should be assumed to derive appropriate requirements.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 401.28 Tm
0.000 0.000 0.000 rg
(Unless specified otherwise for the respective test, the radio conditions on the air interface shall have a block error rate) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 388.88 Tm
(of 0% and the jitter in the IP transport for MTSI-based speech shall be ? 1 ms.) Tj
1 0 0 1 86.20 367.48 Tm
(NOTE 1: For WLAN connections, an RF shielded room may be one way to achieve these requirements on block) Tj
1 0 0 1 128.75 355.08 Tm
(error rate and jitter. Otherwise, care should be taken with potential sources of radio interference and) Tj
1 0 0 1 128.75 342.68 Tm
(their impact.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 321.28 Tm
(In case of MTSI-based speech, the reference client shall allow to synchronize to the clock of the device under test and) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 308.88 Tm
(include a de-jitter buffer to equalize possible jitter in the signal received from the UE.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 287.48 Tm
(When operating with synchronized clock, the de-jitter buffer shall be a static de-jitter buffer and the jitter buffer) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 275.08 Tm
(management shall not compensate for clock skew. The reference client shall not lose or discard packets, shall not) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 262.68 Tm
(trigger retransmission, and shall not use error concealment or time-warping. The initial jitter buffer size \(filling level\)) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 250.28 Tm
(shall be higher than the maximum expected network jitter and the maximum jitter buffer size shall be at least twice) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 237.88 Tm
(the initial size. During jitter buffer reset, the de-jitter buffer shall be emptied/filled to the initial buffer size. In case of) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 225.48 Tm
(buffer over- or underruns, the reference client shall give a warning and it shall be reported.) Tj
1 0 0 1 86.20 204.08 Tm
(NOTE 2: A static de-jitter buffer is a first-in-first-out \(FIFO\) buffer which at the beginning buffers packets until a) Tj
1 0 0 1 128.75 191.68 Tm
(given initial buffer size is reached. Due to changing network delays the filling level of the de-jitter) Tj
1 0 0 1 128.75 179.28 Tm
(buffer can change, but the sum of network delay and jitter buffer delay is constant \(as opposed to an) Tj
1 0 0 1 128.75 166.88 Tm
(adaptive jitter buffer management\). The filling level of the de-jitter buffer represents the de-jitter buffer) Tj
1 0 0 1 128.75 154.48 Tm
(delay.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 133.08 Tm
(For measurements with unsynchronized clock e.g. the measurement of clock skew, jitter buffer over- and underruns) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 120.68 Tm
(cannot be avoided due to the unsynchronized clocks. Under the assumption of jitter-free condition the initial jitter) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 108.28 Tm
(buffer size \(filling level\) shall be chosen such that the maximum clock skew can be compensated without any loss of) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 95.88 Tm
(packets for a given time. For the measurement of clock skews the jitter buffer size should be chosen such that for) Tj
1 0 0 1 72.00 83.48 Tm
(clock skew of up to 100ppm no loss of packets due to buffer over- or under-run shall occur for a sequence of 160s.) Tj
ET
endstream
